5,198,151644,872,198,2610,1231,312,18883,55856,279,3491,323,18130,279,6291,504,279,7167,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $f_1$, $f_2$, $f_3$, $\\dots$ be a sequence of numbers such that\n\\[\n  f_n = f_{n - 1} + f_{n - 2}\n\\]for every integer $n \\ge 3$.  If $f_7 = 83$, what is the sum of the first 10 terms of the sequence?\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve for the sum of the first 10 terms of the sequence \\( f_1, f_2, f_3, \\ldots \\) defined by the recurrence relation \\( f_n = f_{n-1} + f_{n-2} \\) for \\( n \\geq 3 \\), and given that \\( f_7 = 83 \\), we need to determine the values of \\( f_1 \\) and \\( f_2 \\) first.\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"5785e5fb7c4199059b56616e81d5376580758004364498388f38ecc383b58dfe"},"seconds":55.30169653892517,"seed":271581826417444706,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve for the sum of the first 10 terms of the sequence \\( f_1, f_2, f_3, \\ldots \\) defined by the recurrence relation \\( f_n = f_{n-1} + f_{n-2} \\) for \\( n \\geq 3 \\), and given that \\( f_7 = 83 \\), we need to determine the values of \\( f_1 \\) and \\( f_2 \\) first. Let's denote \\( f_1 = a \\) and \\( f_2 = b \\). We can then express the terms of the sequence as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\nf_3 & = f_2 + f_1 = b + a, \\\\\nf_4 & = f_3 + f_2 = (b + a) + b = a + 2b, \\\\\nf_5 & = f_4 + f_3 = (a + 2b) + (b + a) = 2a + 3b, \\\\\nf_6 & = f_5 + f_4 = (2a + 3b) + (a + 2b) = 3a + 5b, \\\\\nf_7 & = f_6 + f_5 = (3a + 5b) + (2a + 3b) = 5a + 8b.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\( f_7 = 83 \\), we have:\n\n\\[\n5a + 8b = 83.\n\\]\n\nWe need to find integer solutions for \\( a \\) and \\( b \\). By inspection, we can try values for \\( a \\) and solve for \\( b \\):\n\nIf \\( a = 3 \\), then:\n\n\\[\n5(3) + 8b = 83 \\implies 15 + 8b = 83 \\implies 8b = 68 \\implies b = 8.5,\n\\]\n\nwhich is not an integer. Trying \\( a = 5 \\):\n\n\\[\n5(5) + 8b = 83 \\implies 25 + 8b = 83 \\implies 8b = 58 \\implies b = 7.25,\n\\]\n\nwhich is also not an integer. Trying \\( a = 1 \\):\n\n\\[\n5(1) + 8b = 83 \\implies 5 + 8b = 83 \\implies 8b = 78 \\implies b = 9.75,\n\\]\n\nwhich is not an integer. Trying \\( a = 7 \\):\n\n\\[\n5(7) + 8b = 83 \\implies 35 + 8b = 83 \\implies 8b = 48 \\implies b = 6,\n\\]\n\nwhich is an integer. So, \\( a = 7 \\) and \\( b = 6 \\) is a valid solution. Now, we can compute the first 10 terms of the sequence:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\nf_1 & = 7, \\\\\nf_2 & = 6, \\\\\nf_3 & = 13, \\\\\nf_4 & = 19, \\\\\nf_5 & = 32, \\\\\nf_6 & = 51, \\\\\nf_7 & = 83, \\\\\nf_8 & = 134, \\\\\nf_9 & = 217, \\\\\nf_{10} & = 351.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nThe sum of the first 10 terms is:\n\n\\[\n7 + 6 + 13 + 19 + 32 + 51 + 83 + 134 + 217 + 351 = 913.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the first 10 terms of the sequence is \\(\\boxed{913}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{913}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve for the sum of the first 10 terms of the sequence \\( f_1, f_2, f_3, \\ldots \\) defined by the recurrence relation \\( f_n = f_{n-1} + f_{n-2} \\) for \\( n \\geq 3 \\), and given that \\( f_7 = 83 \\), we need to determine the values of \\( f_1 \\) and \\( f_2 \\) first. Let's denote \\( f_1 = a \\) and \\( f_2 = b \\). We can then express the terms of the sequence as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\nf_3 & = f_2 + f_1 = b + a, \\\\\nf_4 & = f_3 + f_2 = (b + a) + b = a + 2b, \\\\\nf_5 & = f_4 + f_3 = (a + 2b) + (b + a) = 2a + 3b, \\\\\nf_6 & = f_5 + f_4 = (2a + 3b) + (a + 2b) = 3a + 5b, \\\\\nf_7 & = f_6 + f_5 = (3a + 5b) + (2a + 3b) = 5a + 8b.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\( f_7 = 83 \\), we have:\n\n\\[\n5a + 8b = 83.\n\\]\n\nWe need to find integer solutions for \\( a \\) and \\( b \\). By inspection, we can try values for \\( a \\) and solve for \\( b \\):\n\nIf \\( a = 3 \\), then:\n\n\\[\n5(3) + 8b = 83 \\implies 15 + 8b = 83 \\implies 8b = 68 \\implies b = 8.5,\n\\]\n\nwhich is not an integer. Trying \\( a = 5 \\):\n\n\\[\n5(5) + 8b = 83 \\implies 25 + 8b = 83 \\implies 8b = 58 \\implies b = 7.25,\n\\]\n\nwhich is also not an integer. Trying \\( a = 1 \\):\n\n\\[\n5(1) + 8b = 83 \\implies 5 + 8b = 83 \\implies 8b = 78 \\implies b = 9.75,\n\\]\n\nwhich is not an integer. Trying \\( a = 7 \\):\n\n\\[\n5(7) + 8b = 83 \\implies 35 + 8b = 83 \\implies 8b = 48 \\implies b = 6,\n\\]\n\nwhich is an integer. So, \\( a = 7 \\) and \\( b = 6 \\) is a valid solution. Now, we can compute the first 10 terms of the sequence:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\nf_1 & = 7, \\\\\nf_2 & = 6, \\\\\nf_3 & = 13, \\\\\nf_4 & = 19, \\\\\nf_5 & = 32, \\\\\nf_6 & = 51, \\\\\nf_7 & = 83, \\\\\nf_8 & = 134, \\\\\nf_9 & = 217, \\\\\nf_{10} & = 351.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nThe sum of the first 10 terms is:\n\n\\[\n7 + 6 + 13 + 19 + 32 + 51 + 83 + 134 + 217 + 351 = 913.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the first 10 terms of the sequence is \\(\\boxed{913}\\).","id":"intermediate_algebra:573"}
